\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


A planar point set is in general position if no three points are collinear. A triangulation of it subdivides its convex hull into triangles with that point set as its vertices and with disjoint triangle interiors. Triangulations of $S_1,S_2$ are compatible if a bijection $\phi:S_1\to S_2$ identifies their face lattices; equivalently, a triple spans an empty triangular face in one precisely when its image spans an empty triangular face in the other. The bijection is free to be chosen, and no extra Steiner vertices are added.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
For every two $n$-point planar sets in general position having the same number of convex-hull vertices, do compatible triangulations exist? \href{https://github.com/edemaine/topp/blob/main/Problems/P.000038}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
For every two $n$-point planar sets in general position having the same number of convex-hull vertices, do compatible triangulations exist?

